"journal mode: wal\n\n=== CONCURRENT HISTORY ===\n  time (s)  transaction  op         detail\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  0.001566  txn-1        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.001682  txn-2        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.001953  txn-1        READ       quantity=10 reserved=0 available=10\n  0.002986  txn-2        READ       quantity=10 reserved=0 available=10\n  0.003004  txn-2        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.003054  txn-1        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.203227  txn-2        WRITE      UPDATE reserved 0 -> 6 (based on snapshot available=10)\n  0.203317  txn-1        ABORT      COMMIT rejected (stale snapshot): database is locked; rolled back, no units reserved\n  0.216804  txn-2        COMMIT     committed reserved=6\n  0.221083  system       FINAL-STATE item_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n=== SERIALIZABILITY ANALYSIS ===\n\nTransaction outcomes:\n  txn-1: ABORTED (write rejected, no committed effects)\n  txn-2: COMMITTED\n  -> aborted/rolled-back transactions are logically erased; the dependency graph is built over the committed set ['txn-2'] only.\n\nDependency graph (serialization graph, committed txns):\n  nodes: txn-2\n  edges: (none)\n\nAnomaly checks:\n  lost update : NO — both transactions wrote inventory[item_id=1] from the same snapshot, so the writes conflict; only one committed (first-committer-wins aborted the loser). No committed update was overwritten.\n  write skew  : NO — both transactions' write sets are the same row (inventory[item_id=1]), not disjoint, and they could not both commit; write skew requires two committed transactions with disjoint writes validated against overlapping reads.\n\nVERDICT: dependency graph is ACYCLIC -> the schedule IS conflict-serializable.\nEquivalent serial order of committed transactions: txn-2\n  (txn-1 was aborted/rolled back; its reads observed the pre-write snapshot, consistent with serializing it before txn-2. The committed projection alone is equivalent to the serial history [txn-2].)\n\nWhat snapshot isolation prevented:\n  txn-2 and txn-1 both read inventory[item_id=1] at reserved=0 and both attempted to write it.\n  Had both writes committed, the graph would gain rw anti-dependency edges in both directions (txn-1 -> txn-2 and txn-2 -> txn-1: each read before the other wrote) plus a ww edge — a cycle, i.e. the classic LOST UPDATE.\n  First-committer-wins rejected txn-1's COMMIT (stale snapshot), so the cycle never formed and the committed schedule remains serializable.\n\n=== FINAL COMMITTED STATE ===\nitem_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6\n=== SECOND RUN (insufficient inventory) ===\njournal mode: wal\n\n=== CONCURRENT HISTORY ===\n  time (s)  transaction  op         detail\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  0.000789  txn-1        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.000948  txn-2        BEGIN      deferred transaction started\n  0.001199  txn-1        READ       quantity=10 reserved=6 available=4\n  0.002282  txn-2        READ       quantity=10 reserved=6 available=4\n  0.002299  txn-2        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.002302  txn-2        SKIP       only 4 available (< 6); nothing to reserve\n  0.002325  txn-1        BARRIER    both transactions open and holding their snapshot\n  0.002328  txn-1        SKIP       only 4 available (< 6); nothing to reserve\n  0.002362  txn-1        ROLLBACK   read-only transaction ended\n  0.002383  txn-2        ROLLBACK   read-only transaction ended\n  0.002894  system       FINAL-STATE item_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n=== SERIALIZABILITY ANALYSIS ===\n\nTransaction outcomes:\n  txn-1: ROLLED BACK (read-only, no committed effects)\n  txn-2: ROLLED BACK (read-only, no committed effects)\n  -> aborted/rolled-back transactions are logically erased; the dependency graph is built over the committed set [] only.\n\nDependency graph (serialization graph, committed txns):\n  nodes: (none)\n  edges: (none)\n\nAnomaly checks:\n  lost update : NO — both transactions wrote inventory[item_id=1] from the same snapshot, so the writes conflict; only one committed (first-committer-wins aborted the loser). No committed update was overwritten.\n  write skew  : NO — both transactions' write sets are the same row (inventory[item_id=1]), not disjoint, and they could not both commit; write skew requires two committed transactions with disjoint writes validated against overlapping reads.\n\nVERDICT: no committed transactions — schedule is trivially conflict-serializable.\n\n=== FINAL COMMITTED STATE ===\nitem_id=1 quantity=10 reserved=6"